+
+This document describes how to expose a store to virtual machines with
+`snix store virtiofs`, and how to enable DAX so that guests read file contents
+directly out of the host page cache.
+
+### Run the virtio-fs daemon
+
+`snix store virtiofs` serves a store over a vhost-user socket, which a VMM
+attaches to a guest as a virtio-fs device:
+
+```bash
+snix store virtiofs /run/snix/vm.sock
+```
+
+Point the daemon at whichever services hold your data, the same way as
+`snix store mount`:
+
+```bash
+export BLOB_SERVICE_ADDR=objectstore+file:///var/lib/snix-castore/blobs
+export DIRECTORY_SERVICE_ADDR=redb:///var/lib/snix-castore/directories.redb
+export PATH_INFO_SERVICE_ADDR=redb:///var/lib/snix-store/pathinfo.redb
+```
+
+Each guest needs its own socket, so start one daemon per VM.
+
+### Enable DAX
+
+Without DAX, a guest reading a file sends a request to the daemon, which copies
+the contents into the guest. The bytes then live in both the host page cache and
+the guest's, and every guest pays that cost separately.
+
+With DAX, the guest instead asks the VMM to map the file into a window of guest
+physical memory, and reads it in place. Nothing is copied, and the guest does
+not cache what the host is already caching.
+
+Files are served lazily from a castore and have no descriptor of their own, so
+they must be written somewhere mappable first. Pass a directory to hold them:
+
+```bash
+snix store virtiofs --dax-backing-dir /var/cache/snix-dax /run/snix/vm.sock
+```
+
+Blobs are materialized into that directory on first use and named by their
+digest. Nothing is written there for files a guest never maps.
+
+Your VMM must also be given a DAX window. With [Alioth]:
+
+```bash
+alioth boot ... --fs vu,socket=/run/snix/vm.sock,tag=snix,dax_window=1g
+```
+
+The window is a span of guest address space, not memory that gets allocated, so
+sizing it generously is cheap. Guests mount the filesystem with `-o dax`:
+
+```bash
+mount -t virtiofs -o dax snix /nix/store
+```
+
+DAX requires a guest kernel built with `CONFIG_FUSE_DAX`, which in turn requires
+`CONFIG_FS_DAX`. Distribution kernels do not always enable these. Where the
+guest, the VMM, or the daemon lacks DAX support, mapping requests are declined
+and reads fall back to being copied, so a `--dax-backing-dir` that is never populated
+is a sign that DAX is not actually in use.
+
+### Sharing one cache between VMs
+
+Entries in the cache are named by content digest, so several daemons can be
+pointed at one directory:
+
+```bash
+snix store virtiofs --dax-backing-dir /var/cache/snix-dax /run/snix/vm-a.sock &
+snix store virtiofs --dax-backing-dir /var/cache/snix-dax /run/snix/vm-b.sock &
+```
+
+A blob that two guests both map then resolves to the same host file, and their
+mappings resolve to the same host page cache pages. Shared libraries and other
+files common to many guests are therefore stored, read, and cached once no
+matter how many VMs use them.
+
+This follows from how entries are named rather than from any deduplication
+pass: two guests mapping the same content cannot end up on different pages.
+
+### Lifetime of the cache
+
+Entries are immutable once written and are not removed while the daemon runs, so
+the directory grows to cover the set of blobs guests have actually mapped. It is
+a cache in the sense that deleting it while no VM is running loses nothing but
+the work of writing it again.
+
+Do not delete entries underneath a running VM. A guest may hold mappings into
+them, and unlinking the file will not tear those mappings down.
+
+[Alioth]: https://github.com/google/alioth
